-- title:
--   Gluing three congruent endomorphisms along a flat pushout
-- statement:
--   Let $B$, $B_1$, $B'$, $M$, $N$ be commutative rings in one universe, equipped with $B$-algebra structures on $B_1$, $B'$, $M$, with $B'$- and $M$-algebra structures on $N$ compatible with the $B$-algebra structure on $N$ (scalar towers $B \to B' \to N$ and $B \to M \to N$), with a $B'$-algebra structure on $B_1$, such that $N$ is the pushout of $B' \leftarrow B \rightarrow M$ in the sense of `Algebra.IsPushout B B' M N`, and with $M$ flat as a $B$-module. Assume $\mathrm{algebraMap}\ B\ B_1$ is surjective, and that three $B$-algebra maps $p_0,p_1,p_2 \colon B' \to B$ are given with $\mathrm{algebraMap}\ B\ B_1 \circ p_i = \mathrm{algebraMap}\ B'\ B_1$ for each $i$, jointly injective (if $p_i x = p_i y$ for all $i$ then $x = y$), and jointly surjective in the sense that every triple $b \colon \mathrm{Fin}\ 3 \to B$ whose three images in $B_1$ agree is of the form $(p_i x)_i$ for some $x \in B'$. Assume further three $B$-algebra maps $e_0,e_1,e_2 \colon N \to M$ (slices) with $e_i(\mathrm{algebraMap}\ M\ N\ m) = m$ and $e_i(\mathrm{algebraMap}\ B'\ N\ x) = \mathrm{algebraMap}\ B\ M\ (p_i x)$. Finally let $t_0,t_1,t_2 \colon M \to M$ be $B$-algebra endomorphisms with $t_i m - m \in J M$ for all $m$, where $J M$ denotes the image in $M$ of $\ker(\mathrm{algebraMap}\ B\ B_1)$ under $\mathrm{algebraMap}\ B\ M$. Then there exists a $B'$-algebra endomorphism $\sigma$ of $N$ such that $e_i \circ \sigma = t_i \circ e_i$ for $i = 0,1,2$, such that $\sigma n - n$ lies for every $n$ in the ideal of $N$ generated by the image of $\ker(\mathrm{algebraMap}\ B'\ B_1)$ under $\mathrm{algebraMap}\ B'\ N$, and such that any $B'$-algebra endomorphism $\sigma'$ of $N$ with $e_i \circ \sigma' = t_i \circ e_i$ for all $i$ agrees with $\sigma$ everywhere.
--
--   This is the gluing step for endomorphisms in the setting where $B'$ presents a triple fibre product of $B$ over a quotient $B_1$ and $N = M \otimes_B B'$: three endomorphisms of $M$ that are congruent to the identity modulo the kernel ideal glue to a single endomorphism of $N$, uniquely, and again congruent to the identity. It is used in the construction of endomorphisms of bare deformations compatible with prescribed slices.
